@page "/user-guide"
@using BirkNext.Web.Services

<PageTitle>User Guide — BirkNext</PageTitle>
<div class="ug-page">
    <PageHeader Title="BirkNext User Guide" Description="A practical guide to artifacts, source evidence, applicability, reviews, testing and administration." />
    <nav class="ug-toc" aria-label="User Guide sections">
        @foreach (var link in Sections) { <a class="ug-toc-link" href="#@link.Id">@link.Label</a> }
    </nav>
    <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip"><strong>New to BirkNext?</strong> Bring your own project with <a href="/project-import">Import Project</a>: one ZIP provides both the project documents and the source. For a quick tour, start with a <a href="/sample-projects">Sample Project</a>. Add a Target Environment when you want runtime reviews. <a href="/getting-started">Recommended Workflow</a> shows the next step for your inputs; see <a href="#project-inputs">Project inputs</a>.</div>

    <section id="overview" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">What BirkNext does</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">BirkNext brings together project artifacts, source analysis, traceability, quality reviews and controlled runtime testing. It reports the evidence it has and the limits of that evidence; it does not treat every feature as relevant to every project.</p>
        <div class="ug-cap-grid">
            @foreach (var pillar in Pillars) { <article class="ug-cap-card"><h3 class="ug-cap-name">@pillar.Title</h3><p class="ug-cap-desc">@pillar.Text</p></article> }
        </div>
        <p>Start with the <a href="/getting-started">Recommended Workflow</a> for workspace-specific next steps. The User Guide explains feature ownership and evidence boundaries.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="concepts" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Core concepts</h2>
        <h3>Applicability, coverage and quality</h3><p><strong>Applicable</strong> means a review fits the project. <strong>NotApplicable</strong> (N/A) means it does not. <strong>Unsupported</strong> means the tool cannot assess a relevant technology; it is a tool limitation, not a project quality problem. <strong>NotTested</strong>, <strong>Unavailable</strong>, <strong>NotAssessed</strong> and <strong>No evidence</strong> describe different missing-evidence situations and do not mean failure. Quality scores use assessed checks; coverage is reported separately. Unsupported and N/A work do not lower quality. If nothing was assessed, a score is not inferred.</p>
        <h3>Evidence maturity</h3><p>Evidence has distinct stages: documented → source-confirmed → configured → observed → runtime-verified → executed → passed. A later stage is not implied by an earlier one. A designed test is not an executed test; execution is not a pass. Source evidence does not prove runtime behavior. A pipeline definition shows intended steps, not that a pipeline ran successfully. No automated violation is not full compliance.</p>
        <h3>Source, currentness and provenance</h3><p>A Source Analysis snapshot is an immutable analysis record with a fingerprint, timestamp and provenance. The selected primary snapshot is the source of a review’s main evidence; related snapshots are included only when selected. New analysis creates another snapshot; it does not rewrite history. Current, historical, stale, needs refresh and not comparable are different states. A snapshot is not a deployment.</p>
        <h3>Analyzers, providers, engines and extensions</h3><p>An analyzer interprets source; a provider connects to or observes a system; an engine executes a test; an extension supplies project-specific behavior. Availability does not mean a project uses that capability, and supported does not mean applicable. Azure is one environment provider. Security Classification is currently provided by the M2LB child-security-classification extension.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="getting-started" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Getting started and artifacts</h2>
        <h3 id="project-inputs">Project inputs: documents, source and target</h3>
        <p>BirkNext reviews three kinds of input. Each unlocks different reviews, and none is required for every project.</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Project documents</strong> (recommended start): <a href="/project-import">Import Project</a> is the primary path — one ZIP of the project folder. <a href="/sample-projects">Sample Projects</a> are optional examples, and as an advanced path you can import your own Specification, Constitution, Plan, Tasks, Data Model or Research documents in any explorer. They populate the shared artifact repository of the current workspace. Document Quality Review and Requirements Traceability read them.</li>
            <li><strong>Source</strong> (optional, for source-based reviews): the same <a href="/project-import">Import Project</a> ZIP creates the Source Analysis snapshot, so the archive is never uploaded twice; a source-only archive can also be uploaded directly in <a href="/source-analysis">Source Analysis</a>. The snapshot feeds Technology Coverage, Dependency Review, Pipeline Review and Environment Analysis. No Target Environment is needed: a source snapshot belongs to the workspace, not to a target.</li>
            <li><strong>Target Environment</strong> (required for runtime reviews only): set the application URL, authentication, endpoints and integrations in System Settings › Target Environments (sidebar: <em>Project Inputs › Target Environments</em>). Frontend, API, Integration and Performance reviews and Critical E2E Regression run against it. An environment with no URL, or with the example URL it was created with, is <em>Partial</em>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Recommended Workflow shows the three inputs with their state (<em>Not provided</em>, <em>Available</em>/<em>Analyzed</em>/<em>Configured</em>, <em>Partial</em>, <em>Needs refresh</em>, <em>Selection required</em>) and recommends the next action from what is actually open: with nothing provided it suggests importing the project (or loading a Sample Project) and offers source and target as alternatives. A document-only project never waits for source or a target, a source-only project never waits for documents, and input completeness is not a quality score.</p>
        <p>The sidebar follows the same flow: <em>Getting Started</em> (with the cross-cutting Dashboard), <em>Project Inputs</em> (<em>Import Project</em> first, then the optional <em>Sample Projects</em>, then <em>Target Environments</em> — runtime configuration of the deployed system, not a project document input), then the pages that consume them: <em>Document Review</em>, <em>Traceability</em>, <em>Source Review</em> (starting with <em>Source Analysis</em>, which inspects the source snapshot an import created), <em>Quality &amp; Testing</em> and <em>Extensions</em> (project-specific reviews such as Security Classification), with System Settings under <em>Admin</em>.</p>
        <h3 id="project-import">Import Project: one archive, two evidence domains</h3>
        <p><a href="/project-import">Import Project</a> takes one ZIP of the project folder (up to 50 MB, the same safety limits as Source Analysis: no traversal, absolute or UNC paths, no symbolic links, bounded entry count and expanded size). The archive is uploaded and validated once, then BirkNext shows what it found before anything is imported: the project documents by artifact role, and whether it contains source (code, project files, dependency manifests, pipelines, infrastructure as code, contracts) with the technologies detected. Import project then populates two separate owners from that same archive:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Project artifacts</strong>: detected documents become artifact revisions of the imported project in the shared artifact repository, found by the same generic role discovery as Sample Projects. A role with one document is selected; a role with several keeps all of them and you choose in its Explorer. Every role is optional.</li>
            <li><strong>Source</strong>: the source becomes an immutable Source Analysis snapshot of the workspace — no Target Environment needed. Source Analysis shows it as the current snapshot right away — no second upload.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Both remain separate evidence domains with their own lifecycle; they share only the import identity and the archive fingerprint (SHA-256 of the exact uploaded bytes), which both pages show. Imported is not reviewed, approved or passed. A documents-only archive (<em>No source detected</em>) and a source-only archive (<em>No supported project artifacts detected</em>) are complete, neutral outcomes. <em>Imported with notes</em> means something is partial — a role to choose, documents that could not be classified or read, technology Source Analysis does not analyze, or a snapshot that was not created yet — never a failure. The previewed archive is staged on the backend for 30 minutes and survives a backend restart, so <em>Import project</em> still works without choosing the ZIP again; after that the preview expires and the page asks you to re-import. No Target Environment is needed for either part: the import shows the target as <em>Not configured — required only for runtime reviews</em>, and a target added, switched or deleted later uses the same snapshot without importing again. Only a genuine source failure (analysis or save) offers a retry from the staged archive (kept for 30 minutes) without choosing the ZIP again. A rejected archive activates nothing.</p>
        <p>The imported project becomes the current project and replaces a selected Sample Project. Documents of another import, of a Sample Project or of the manual workspace are never mixed in; earlier imports and snapshots stay as history, and <em>Close imported project</em> returns to the manual workspace. Importing the same archive again is recognized by its fingerprint: its documents are not duplicated, and when its snapshot is still the current one it is reused rather than analyzed again. A different archive (a new version) creates a new import and a new current snapshot. The client file name is shown as metadata only; no local path is used or stored.</p>
        <p>Use <a href="/sample-projects">Sample Projects</a> to load a ready-made example project’s available artifacts and project context. Sample Projects are predefined examples; they are not needed for your own imported project. Loading a sample interacts with the local project/workspace state; it does not imply that every sample artifact or review applies. You can also work with document-only projects.</p>
        <h3 id="sample-project-discovery">How sample documents are found</h3>
        <p>BirkNext scans each sample project folder recursively (build, dependency and tool folders such as <code>bin</code>, <code>obj</code>, <code>node_modules</code> and <code>.git</code> are skipped; links are not followed; very large or binary files are not read) and classifies every Markdown document by artifact role: Constitution, Specification, Data Model, Plan, Tasks or Research. The role comes, in order of precedence, from front matter (for example <code>type: plan</code>), an exact canonical filename such as <code>spec.md</code>, and the document’s structure as read by the same parsers the Explorers use. Canonical filenames are strong hints, not requirements: <code>docs/requirements/attendance.md</code> or <code>.specify/memory/constitution.md</code> work as well. Folder names and filename synonyms only support a role the content already suggests.</p>
        <p>Every role is optional. A project without a constitution, plan or tasks is not incomplete; that role shows <em>Not found</em> and the matching Explorer says no artifact of that role was detected in the project. A role can have several documents; all are kept and listed, and you choose on the project card, or in the Explorer’s artifact selector, which one the Explorer opens. BirkNext never picks one arbitrarily. Documents that fit two roles about equally, or only weakly, are shown under <em>Needs review</em> and are not opened by Explorers. Documents without a supported role, such as READMEs, checklists and contracts, are listed under <em>Other documents</em>. <em>Could not be read</em> is a read error, which is different from a missing document. Use <em>Why detected?</em> and <em>View All Files</em> to see the reasons.</p>
        <p>Selecting a project stores only its identity in the shared artifact repository. The Explorers, Document Quality Review and traceability pages resolve the project’s documents by role when they open. They never copy them into the session, and discovery never changes sample files.</p>
        <p id="current-workspace"><strong>One current workspace for every page.</strong> The Dashboard, Recommended Workflow, the Explorers and the sidebar read the same current workspace: the selected Sample Project’s documents, documents imported into the workspace, or a resumed saved workspace. A workspace is loaded as soon as one of these exists, even before it is saved; <em>Not saved</em>, <em>Auto-saved</em> and <em>Saved</em> describe saving only. A workspace without a project (imports only) shows <em>Project: Not assigned</em>. Pages count artifact <em>roles</em> (“5 artifact roles available”); several documents of one role add to the artifact count, not the role count, and every role is optional. Availability, selection, analysis and review are separate: an available artifact that no analysis has read says <em>Not analyzed</em>, never <em>Not loaded</em>. A score with nothing assessed yet, such as Release Readiness before any review decision, shows “—”, not 0%.</p>
        <p id="review-decisions"><strong>Review decisions apply to an artifact revision.</strong> In Recommended Workflow each review step says where it stands: <em>Ready to review</em> (the artifact is available, nobody has reviewed it), <em>Reviewed</em> (inspected, no approval implied), <em>Approved</em>, <em>Needs changes</em>, <em>Review stale</em> (the artifact changed since the last decision), <em>Blocked</em> (choose an artifact, or approve an earlier step first) or <em>N/A</em> (the step needs a document this project does not have). Record a decision under <em>Record review decision</em>; it names the exact file and content fingerprint it applies to. Opening an explorer records nothing. When the document changes, the earlier decision stays in history but no longer counts, and the step is recommended again. Another document of the same role never inherits a decision. <em>Manual Review</em> counts the required steps that apply; <em>What Manual Review counts</em> lists them, and optional steps such as the Data Model review are shown but not counted. No document is required of every project. On the Dashboard, <em>Governance</em> shows “—” (<em>Not assessed</em>) until such a decision exists; the number of available artifact roles is context, not a governance score.</p>
        <p>The Constitution, Specification, Data Model, Plan and Tasks explorers present their respective artifacts. Specification identifiers can be project-defined (for example, JIRA-123 or US-A1); do not assume a single ID scheme. Requirements Traceability presents the shared relationship graph across requirements, plans, tasks, implementation evidence and test evidence. A designed test and a recorded execution remain separate evidence.</p>
        <p>Specification Explorer's tree shows structure parsed from the selected Specification. Deterministic review analysis runs automatically for the current artifact; you can choose <em>Re-analyze</em> to run it again. Traceability and coverage are separate assessments in Requirements Traceability; a requirement or test parsed from the document is not, by itself, evidence of coverage.</p>
        <p id="task-explorer"><strong>Task Explorer keeps five facts apart.</strong> <em>Marked done in Task artifact</em> (“64 / 65 (98%)”) counts checked boxes ([x]) in the Task document: it is not implementation or test completion. <em>Planning</em> counts parallelizable tasks (marked [P] in the Task document), the same list the Parallelizable view shows; it is structure, not progress. <em>Traceability</em> reads “39 with traceability links · 19 with no traceability links”: a link is an FR, SC or user-story reference, a matched specification item or a traceability-table row, and the two numbers always add up to the task total. The summary, the <em>With traceability links</em> / <em>No traceability links</em> filters and the tree use the same rule. <em>Implementation evidence</em> and <em>Test evidence</em> come from the SDD lifecycle that Implementation Review records, per requirement, so a task shows the evidence of the requirements it references: Current, Stale or None for implementation; designed, executed, passed or failed for tests. Without Source Analysis or recorded test evidence they say <em>Not assessed</em> and why, never “missing” or zero; test results are imported in Implementation Review. A task’s details group <em>Status</em> (only the Task artifact’s own state), <em>Task topics</em> (Parallelizable, Testing, Security, Architecture: found in the task’s wording and markers, never links or results), and then <em>Traceability</em>, <em>Test assets</em> and <em>Implementation</em>. A task that mentions testing is a testing topic, not a linked test asset. Long task text wraps in the details panel, with Show more for very long tasks.</p>
        <p><strong>Explorers read artifact roles from the current workspace.</strong> Each Explorer lists the artifacts that have its role: the selected Sample Project’s detected documents, plus documents imported into the same workspace with the Explorer’s <em>Import</em> action (file picker, drag and drop, or pasted text). No Sample Project needs to be selected; imports without a project form the manual workspace. File names such as <code>spec.md</code> or <code>constitution.md</code> are common examples, not requirements. <code>requirements.md</code> imported as a Specification opens in the Specification Explorer. Each artifact shows its title, file name, source and, for imports, its revision. An import is kept as an artifact revision of the shared repository and is saved with the workspace. It starts without authority and never creates a baseline. Imports belong to the project they were made in and never appear in another project.</p>
        <p>Every Explorer, Document Quality Review, Requirements Traceability and Implementation Review distinguish three situations. With <em>no project loaded</em> (no imported project, no Sample Project and no imported document) they offer <em>Import Project</em> first, <em>Load Sample Project</em> second and a single-role import as the advanced path. When a project is loaded but a role has several candidates, they say the role <em>needs selection</em> and list the candidates — never “no artifact”. When the project has no candidate for a role, they say it was not detected, neutrally. When a role has several artifacts, the Explorer opens the one you selected (in the Explorer, on Sample Projects, or by importing it), otherwise the only Approved/Baseline artifact, otherwise the only artifact. If none of these applies, it asks you to choose. It never opens the first file found and never merges documents. Selecting an artifact for viewing does not change its authority. Historical and superseded artifacts are labelled. <em>Analysis failed</em> means the artifact is loaded but could not be parsed. An error loading the project’s documents is shown as an error, never as “no artifact”.</p>
        <p>Artifact governance includes revisions, fingerprints, authority, baselines, supersession, questions, decisions, traceability and stale or historical evidence where captured. These features do not amount to a complete lifecycle or approval/sign-off system.</p>
        <p id="specification-review"><strong>Specification Explorer counts entities, not candidates.</strong> <em>Requirements</em>, <em>Tests</em> and <em>Clarifications analyzed</em> count unique logical items of the analyzed Specification (one FR-004, however often it is mentioned); a test or clarification related to a requirement never adds a requirement. <em>Review candidates</em> are the analyzer’s suggestions for human review, counted separately and filterable by candidate type, with search and a short preview (<em>Show all</em>, <em>Show less</em>); a candidate is not a finding, a defect or a failed requirement. <em>Primary context</em> places each item under one parent for navigation, only from document structure (the section it sits in) or an explicit identifier it names, never from similar wording. Traceability links are kept separately and can be many-to-many: a test that names FR-004 and FR-007 is counted once, verifies both, and stays under its user story. Clarification links never count as test coverage, and none of these relationships sets Traceability &amp; Coverage, which stays <em>Not assessed</em> until Requirements Traceability assesses it. Given, When and Then are shown on separate lines in blue, indigo and teal; red is reserved for failures and errors.</p>
        <p><a href="/plan-explorer">Plan Explorer</a> organizes declared phases and decisions. A normalized phase key helps compare data; it is not a guarantee that each source declaration is unique.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="source-analysis" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Source Analysis</h2>
        <p><a href="/source-analysis">Source Analysis</a> accepts safe ZIP repositories up to 50 MB compressed and 100 MB expanded, including nested folders, multiple top-level services, document-only archives and technologies it cannot analyze. Unsupported technology is reported as an analysis limitation, not an invalid archive. ZIPs must not contain traversal paths or symbolic links; archives are read in memory and are not extracted to disk. Source Analysis does not require a Target Environment: snapshots belong to the workspace, and a target is runtime context needed only for deployed/runtime checks. Other reviews select existing snapshots; they do not need a duplicate source ZIP upload. <a href="/project-import">Import Project</a> creates the current snapshot automatically from the project ZIP and the snapshot says so (<em>Created by: Project Import</em>, with the import identity); <em>Upload source only</em> remains for source-only archives or to analyze the same source again.</p>
        <p>Current areas include:</p><ul class="ug-fd-list">@foreach (var domain in DocumentationCatalog.DocumentedSourceAnalysisDomains) { <li>@domain</li> }</ul>
        <p>Source Analysis describes source declarations and analyzer findings. It does not establish deployment state, runtime behavior, successful pipeline execution or contract use in production.</p>
        <p>Each snapshot has a fingerprint and provenance. Re-analysis produces a new snapshot. Consumers keep the exact snapshot scope used; a newer snapshot may be offered, but evidence is not silently switched. Old analyzer evidence can need refresh when source changes.</p>
        <h3 id="technology-coverage">Technology Coverage</h3><p><a href="/technology-coverage">Technology Coverage</a> shows detected technologies and the dimensions BirkNext can support, such as configuration, source analysis, contracts, runtime observation, active tests and test plans. Support may be full, partial, unsupported or not applicable by dimension. Unsupported means an analyzer/provider/tool limitation; it does not reduce project quality. Use this page for current support claims rather than inferring capability from a technology name or integration type.</p>
        <h3 id="environment-analysis">Environment Analysis</h3><p><a href="/azure-environment">Environment Analysis</a> is a provider-backed environment inventory. The current provider is Azure; its presence or detection does not make BirkNext Azure-only, nor prove the project uses Azure. Provider details are adapters and optional enrichment.</p>
        <h3 id="generated-documentation">Generated documentation</h3><p>Some projects keep documentation that a tool, script, build step or agent workflow writes from the code (for example per-module <code>autodoc/</code> or <code>generated-docs/</code> folders with an overview, architecture, data model, messaging, OpenAPI or GraphQL files). Source Analysis records it as its own evidence type: authored documentation (Constitution, Specification, Plan, Tasks, Data Model) ≠ generated documentation ≠ source evidence ≠ runtime evidence. A folder is generated when a workflow file declares it as an output (its generator is named only when the workflow names itself) or by a folder convention; without a workflow it is shown as “Likely generated / Unknown provenance”. Freshness is judged per module from dates the documents declare and dated source evidence of the same module (such as migration identifiers, or archive times when they vary); uniform archive times never make documentation stale, so the result can be Current, Potentially stale or Unknown. <a href="/admin/system-settings?section=generated-docs-health">Generated Documentation Health</a> (System Settings → Developer) compares structured keys only — routes, messaging resources, entities, configuration keys, component names and contract operations — with source, contracts and the selected authored artifacts of the same imported project. Every discrepancy is “Potential drift – requires review”: source is stronger implementation evidence, a generated document never supersedes the specification, never feeds an explorer, and never verifies implementation or a completed task. No discrepancy found does not mean the documentation is fully synchronized.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="traceability" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Traceability and implementation evidence</h2>
        <p><a href="/artifact-traceability">Requirements Traceability</a> presents the shared SDD graph: requirement → plan → task → implementation evidence → designed test → test execution. Links and evidence may be partial or stale; the graph does not turn source links into runtime verification.</p>
        <p id="implementation-review"><a href="/task-alignment">Implementation Review</a> analyzes every task in the Task artifact against the Specification with deterministic rules. It reads both artifacts from the current workspace — an imported project, a Sample Project or the manual workspace — and names the role that still needs selection when a project has several candidates; a result is bound to the workspace and to both artifacts’ fingerprints, so another project or a changed document never shows an earlier result as current. Each task gets one result: <em>Direct spec link</em> (an FR, SC or user-story reference that resolves in the Specification — a link, not coverage or proof of implementation), <em>Technical only</em> (setup, infrastructure or test work where a direct spec link is not expected), <em>Needs review</em> or <em>Possible deviation</em>. Only the last two are findings. Test priority on the Risk &amp; testing tab comes from keyword topics (security, events, ingestion …); it points testing effort and is not a measured risk. Results are bound to the Specification and Task artifact fingerprints and show Current, Stale (an artifact changed — Re-analyze) or Not run. This page does not read source code, so it is not full implementation verification; source and test evidence is in <a href="/implementation-review">Implementation Evidence Review</a>.</p>
        <p><a href="/implementation-traceability">Implementation Traceability</a> records the current work-item-to-change evidence flow. It is distinct from Implementation Review’s plan/task alignment view. Availability of provider metadata does not mean a work item, pull request or commit is verified beyond the evidence displayed by the page.</p>
        <p>Imported test results such as TRX are execution records. Source-discovered test definitions, planned tests, execution records, results and requirement links remain separate. A result can become stale relative to changed requirements or source.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="quality-reviews" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Quality reviews</h2>
        <p><a href="/quality/document">Document Quality Review</a> evaluates project artifacts in the shared artifact repository and runs without a source snapshot. Its QA Auditor, Data Model Quality, Constitution Compliance, WCAG 2.2 — Documentation, OWASP Security Documentation Coverage, GDPR Documentation Coverage, ISO 25010 — Documented Quality Characteristics, QA Readiness and Delivery Readiness packs inspect documents only. Standards results describe documentation coverage, not WCAG conformance, source or runtime security, legal compliance, measured ISO quality, or deployed database correctness.</p>
        <p>Source Analysis is the source evidence provider and inspection surface. Technology Coverage, Dependency Review, Pipeline Review and Environment Analysis remain specialist source evidence pages; there is no generic Source Quality Review. One Project Import can provide both project artifacts and a Source Analysis snapshot.</p>
        <p>Frontend Quality Review, API Quality Review and Integration Quality Review consume supported target, runtime, configuration and source evidence. These evidence types remain distinct: browser findings do not prove full WCAG conformance, source configuration does not prove runtime enforcement, and configured integrations do not prove successful runtime flow.</p>
        <p>Where a stable domain rule has a defensible standards relationship, the review attaches a reference to that existing finding. For example, axe rule metadata can map a browser accessibility finding to a WCAG criterion. The reference does not add another finding or establish WCAG conformance, OWASP compliance, GDPR legal compliance or ISO certification. Document standards rules are never rerun by FQR/AQR/IQR; unsupported relationships remain unmapped.</p>
        <p>Document Quality Review shows shared SDD lifecycle evidence as context; it is not silently included as a pack finding. Artifact <em>Available</em> means present, not reviewed or approved. Pack readiness describes input availability, not the eventual result.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="pipeline" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Pipeline Review</h2>
        <p><a href="/pipeline-review">Pipeline Review</a> interprets CI/CD definitions owned and captured by Source Analysis. Its Overview, Flow, Gaps, Tests, Environments, Dependencies and Changes views describe declared pipeline structure and evidence. A detected definition does not mean the pipeline executed, passed or deployed.</p>
        <p><strong>Ready</strong>, <strong>Partial</strong>, <strong>Needs refresh</strong>, <strong>Unsupported</strong> and <strong>N/A</strong> retain distinct meanings. Needs refresh means source evidence is outdated for the current comparison; it is not N/A. Azure DevOps metadata can enrich a review but is optional for source-definition review.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="testing" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Runtime, browser and performance testing</h2>
        <p>Target Environment settings define target characteristics, authentication, observed endpoints, browser evidence and expectations. Endpoint Discovery owns observed network/API endpoint evidence. Browser Discovery owns Browser Companion sessions and raw browser evidence. Frontend Quality Review interprets browser evidence. Critical E2E Regression covers configured browser journeys and their safety-gated execution; it is not a general proof of application correctness.</p>
        <h3>Authentication: configuration, verification and prerequisites</h3><p>The Authentication tab keeps four answers apart. <strong>Configuration</strong> is the saved provider and identifiers; detection that matches it is source evidence, not verification. <strong>Verification</strong> is a check of the sign-in workflow and becomes stale when the configuration changes. The <strong>verification prerequisites</strong> are the HTTPS inspection certificate (installation state in the Windows user certificate store), the Local HTTPS Proxy (a runtime BirkNext starts and stops on request) and the dedicated Edge browser (opened with the proxy's port). The <strong>testing context</strong> is what a running proxy session has captured. Saving or applying authentication never installs, removes or forgets the certificate and never stops the proxy; when the proxy is running for the previous configuration it shows Restart required. A stopped proxy is "Proxy not started", not a fault. Reset Local Data stops the project-bound proxy session and keeps the certificate, port configuration and proxy capability.</p>
        <h3>Performance Test Review and engines</h3><p><a href="/performance-test-review">Performance Test Review</a> manages a project’s performance test definition, scenario, workload, thresholds, resources, readiness, run, results, comparison and history. Supported workload types are shown by the configured engine. System Settings → Performance Test Engines reports host capability: k6 readiness, Podman runtime/image availability, supported workload capabilities and resource providers. The k6 container is on-demand rather than part of the BirkNext image where the current engine configuration says so.</p>
        <p>A performance baseline is explicitly selected and can change. The previous baseline remains historical; the latest run does not become baseline automatically. Drift comparisons require comparable scenarios and evidence. Resource Stability is not a memory-leak detector: it evaluates available Podman resource-provider data and BirkNext API runtime metrics. It cannot infer external .NET process or browser memory behavior absent implemented providers (such as OpenTelemetry/Application Insights). States include StableWithinPolicy, PotentialRegression, Regression, InsufficientEvidence, Unavailable and NotComparable.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="ai-generated-code-review" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">AI-Generated Code Review</h2><p><a href="/ai-generated-code-review">AI-Generated Code Review</a> (Quality &amp; Testing) reviews ordinary source code that was generated or heavily changed with coding assistants for risk patterns that are common after such changes. <strong>It does not detect whether AI wrote the code</strong> and never claims so: you choose the profile, and it evaluates project evidence. The review is deterministic — no language model is called, no API key is needed and there is no score.</p><p><strong>Workflow:</strong> Import Project (or Source Analysis) → optionally load the Specification and Constitution → choose the current Source Analysis snapshot → optionally choose a baseline snapshot of the same repository → Run → inspect findings → run the runtime reviews (API, Frontend, Integration Quality Review) where runtime behaviour matters. Source is never uploaded on this page.</p><p><strong>What it checks</strong> (each rule has an id such as AIC-SEC-001 and reports Executed, Not applicable, Not assessed, Unsupported or Failed): requirements alignment from the existing requirement graph (task marked done ≠ implemented; a link ≠ coverage); unresolved project namespace imports and undeclared configuration keys (a build is the confirmation); duplicate types and method bodies by normalized structure; introduced dependencies without usage evidence; authorization removed between snapshots or missing among protected siblings (source metadata — runtime enforcement is not verified), permissive CORS and unconditional developer exception pages; empty catches, broad catches returning success and exception details returned to clients; weak or skipped tests and test-change gaps where the mapping is reliable; NotImplementedException, TODO stubs and placeholder values (comment-only TODOs are informational); unused private methods; contract and configuration changes between snapshots (changed ≠ incompatible ≠ consumer impact); and generated documentation not refreshed.</p><p><strong>Limits:</strong> source-level rules exist for C# only — other languages are listed as unsupported, never as clean; snapshots analyzed before this profile existed need analyzing again; architecture rules are only evaluated when they are explicit (none are invented); mirror-test detection is unsupported. “No indicators observed by the checks executed” never means the code is correct. Each run is stored with the exact snapshot ids it reviewed and exported as HTML without source text.</p></section>
    <section id="system-settings" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">System Settings and administration</h2>
        <p>System Settings contains operational controls and diagnostics. Configuration health is about configuration, not project quality. Feature Visibility controls menu availability; visibility does not imply capability support or project applicability.</p>
        <div class="ug-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="System Settings documentation table" tabindex="0"><table class="ug-table"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Purpose and audience</th><th>Safety meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        @foreach (var row in DocumentationCatalog.DocumentedSettings) { <tr><th scope="row">@row.Name</th><td>@row.Purpose</td><td>@row.Safety</td></tr> }
        </tbody></table></div>
        <h3>Target Environments</h3><p>Target Environments are configuration scoped to a target, separate from project source evidence. The tabs have distinct owners:</p>
        <div class="ug-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Target Environment tabs table" tabindex="0"><table class="ug-table"><thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>Owns</th></tr></thead><tbody>@foreach (var tab in DocumentationCatalog.DocumentedTargetTabs) { <tr><th scope="row">@tab.Name</th><td>@tab.Purpose</td></tr> }</tbody></table></div>
        <p>Project Compatibility is available in System Settings under Developer diagnostics. It runs deterministic generated archive scenarios against the same bounded archive reader and artifact/source discovery used by Project Import. The diagnostic stays in memory and does not import a project or change the current workspace, artifact choices, Source Analysis snapshot, Target Environment or saved review results. A pass means discovery handled the tested portability case; it is not a project quality pass. Automated tests remain the authoritative CI checks. A local real-project acceptance archive is optional and is not required for normal use or CI.</p>
        <p>Performance Test Engines describes host capability; Performance Test Review describes a project’s test configuration and executions. Environment Diagnostics, System Diagnostics, Project Compatibility, ReviewContext Validation, Runtime Diagnostics and Documentation Health are admin/developer diagnostic tools, not project quality reviews. Reset Local Data clears workspaces and SDD artifacts, Source Analysis snapshots/evidence, Target Environments (restored to generic defaults), integration catalogs and applied templates, review/test results, performance definitions/history, Critical E2E flows and cached browser project state. Installation-level configuration/provider capabilities and external files are preserved by the current reset contract. Read the confirmation scope before resetting; reset is irreversible.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="feature-matrix" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Feature Matrix</h2><p>Navigation coverage is tied to stable route-derived IDs, not changing display labels. Applicability is project-specific.</p>
        <div class="ug-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Feature matrix, scroll horizontally for all columns" tabindex="0"><table class="ug-table"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Navigation area</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Evidence and inputs</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Applicability</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        @foreach (var feature in DocumentationCatalog.Features) { var detail = DocumentationCatalog.DetailFor(feature.FeatureId); <tr><th scope="row"><a href="@feature.Route">@feature.DisplayName</a><br /><a href="#@feature.UserGuideAnchor">Guide section</a></th><td>@feature.Category</td><td>@detail.Purpose</td><td>@detail.EvidenceAndInputs</td><td>@detail.Boundary</td><td>@(feature.ExtensionScope is not null ? $"Extension: {feature.ExtensionScope}" : feature.ProviderScope is not null ? $"Provider: {feature.ProviderScope}" : !feature.Required ? "Optional; hidden unless enabled" : "Check project applicability and evidence")</td></tr> }
        </tbody></table></div>
    </section>

    <section id="workflows" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Typical workflows</h2>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list"><li><strong>Document-only:</strong> choose a Sample Project or import documents → explorers → Requirements Traceability → applicable Document Quality Review packs. No source or target needed.</li><li><strong>Source-only:</strong> import the project ZIP or analyze source once (no Target Environment needed) → Technology Coverage → Dependency, Pipeline and Environment reviews where applicable; each review selects the exact snapshot it consumes.</li><li><strong>Target/runtime:</strong> configure a Target Environment → collect Endpoint/Browser evidence where appropriate → API or Frontend Quality Review → safe Critical E2E journey if needed.</li><li><strong>Integrations:</strong> configure integrations → review source/runtime evidence and support → Integration Quality Review and Message Flow Review where supported.</li><li><strong>Performance:</strong> configure target expectations → inspect Performance Test Engines → define and run Performance Test Review → explicitly select a baseline for comparison.</li><li><strong>Pipeline:</strong> analyze source CI/CD definitions → Pipeline Review → refresh source analysis when evidence is stale.</li></ul>
        <p><strong>Full project:</strong> documents, source and a target together give the fullest set of reviews; the next step then comes from open review work, not from input setup.</p>
        <p>Use the live <a href="/getting-started">Recommended Workflow</a> for the current workspace’s next action rather than treating this reference as a mandatory sequence.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="where" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Where do I find this?</h2><div class="ug-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Navigation reference table" tabindex="0"><table class="ug-table"><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Open</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        @foreach (var row in WhereRows) { <tr><th scope="row">@row.Need</th><td><a href="@row.Route">@row.Location</a></td></tr> }
    </tbody></table></div></section>

    <section id="scenarios" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Helpful scenarios</h2><ul class="ug-fd-list">
        <li><strong>“I loaded source—what next?”</strong> Check Source Analysis snapshot/provenance, then Technology Coverage and only applicable consumers.</li><li><strong>“My project uses Java/AWS.”</strong> Check Technology Coverage dimensions. Provider/analyzer gaps are Unsupported or Partial tool limitations, not a project failure.</li><li><strong>“I have documents but no source.”</strong> Use explorers, Requirements Traceability and applicable Document Quality Review packs; source-dependent evidence remains absent.</li><li><strong>“Pipeline Review says Needs refresh.”</strong> Re-analyze the changed source and select the appropriate current snapshot; stale does not mean not applicable.</li><li><strong>“I see No evidence.”</strong> The product lacks evidence to assess; it is not a failed check.</li><li><strong>“I need to load test an API.”</strong> Check engine readiness, define a safe scenario/workload/threshold, review readiness, then run and inspect results.</li><li><strong>“I need to reset local data.”</strong> Read Maintenance’s exact confirmation scope before using Reset Local Data.</li><li><strong>“I imported TRX.”</strong> Treat it as execution evidence; confirm correlation to the intended test, requirement and source before relying on it.</li>
    </ul></section>

    <section id="discovery" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Feature discovery</h2><p>Navigation badges describe applicability or evidence state: Partial means only part is assessed; Setup means configuration is needed; Needs refresh means evidence is stale; No evidence means there is not enough evidence; N/A means not applicable; Unsupported means a tool limitation; Extension identifies project-specific extension behavior. A provider badge such as Azure identifies the provider, not a universal BirkNext dependency. Badges are not quality grades.</p><p id="backend-connectivity"><strong>Backend connectivity, feature endpoints and configuration are separate.</strong> When a page cannot load something it says which request failed, in that section only, with <em>Check again</em> for that request and <em>Technical details</em> (operation, route, HTTP status, category — never tokens, identities or query values). <em>Backend reachable</em> means the backend answered with an HTTP status: a 400, 401, 403, 404 or 500 is a failure of that feature endpoint (request rejected, authentication required, access denied, endpoint not found, server error), not an outage, and the rest of the page keeps working. <em>Backend unreachable</em> is shown only when no HTTP response arrived (connection refused, host not found, network failure, or a response the browser blocked under its cross-origin policy); <em>Request timed out</em>, <em>Secure connection failed</em> and <em>Cancelled</em> are reported as such. No Target Environment selected, Browser Companion not connected, Build not set, no Source Analysis snapshot and runtime test context not configured are configuration or evidence states, never backend errors. Every page uses the one <code>BackendUrl</code> configured for the web app.</p></section>
    <section id="validation" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Documentation and product synchronization</h2><p>Documentation Health compares the current navigation catalog and routable pages with stable documentation feature IDs, guide anchors, System Settings sections, Target Environment tabs and Source Analysis domains. When a feature is added or renamed, update its stable ID metadata, guide coverage and links. Deterministic checks validate route and coverage consistency; they do not claim to prove that prose is complete or accurate.</p></section>
    <section id="faq" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">FAQ</h2><ul class="ug-fd-list"><li><strong>What does Unsupported mean?</strong> A tool/provider cannot assess that dimension; it is not a project quality finding.</li><li><strong>Why N/A?</strong> The review does not apply to this project. N/A differs from unavailable or unsupported.</li><li><strong>Source vs runtime evidence?</strong> Source describes declarations; runtime evidence is observed or executed behavior in a target.</li><li><strong>What is a performance baseline?</strong> An explicitly selected historical result used for comparable drift analysis; newest does not become baseline automatically.</li><li><strong>Why is Security Classification an extension?</strong> The current feature is the M2LB child-security-classification extension, not a generic product security assessment.</li><li><strong>Why is a feature visible if unsupported?</strong> Visibility is navigation policy; support and project applicability are separate evaluations.</li><li><strong>How do I know which reviews apply?</strong> Use the applicability state and reason, then check Technology Coverage and the target/evidence prerequisites.</li></ul></section>
    <section id="glossary" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Glossary</h2><p><strong>Artifact:</strong> project document. <strong>Revision:</strong> recorded version. <strong>Fingerprint:</strong> content identity. <strong>Baseline:</strong> explicitly selected comparison result. <strong>Superseded:</strong> replaced for current use but retained historically. <strong>Current/Historical/Stale:</strong> evidence time relationship. <strong>Review Pack:</strong> selected set of Quality Review checks. <strong>Applicability:</strong> whether a review fits a project. <strong>Coverage:</strong> assessed scope, distinct from quality. <strong>Source Snapshot:</strong> immutable analyzed source record. <strong>Primary/Related Source:</strong> main snapshot and explicitly included supporting snapshots. <strong>Analyzer/Provider/Engine/Extension:</strong> source interpreter, integration/observer, test executor, or project-specific capability. <strong>Target Environment:</strong> configured target profile. <strong>Endpoint/Browser Discovery:</strong> observed network evidence / browser companion evidence. <strong>Runtime Evidence:</strong> observations or test execution against a target. <strong>Scenario/Workload/Threshold:</strong> test journey / load shape / expected limit. <strong>Drift:</strong> change relative to a comparable baseline. <strong>Pipeline Evidence:</strong> source-declared automation. <strong>TRX:</strong> test execution result format. <strong>CodeLink:</strong> source reference link. <strong>Implementation Evidence:</strong> evidence connecting work to code. <strong>Designed Test/Executed Test:</strong> planned case / recorded run.</p></section>
    <section id="ecosystem" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Ecosystem and limitations</h2><p>BirkNext works with project documents, source code, configured target systems, optional providers, test engines and runtime evidence. Cloud providers and project extensions are optional, scoped capabilities. BirkNext is evidence-driven: no source evidence proves runtime behavior; browser automation cannot prove everything; active tests are safety-gated; performance testing is controlled; and unsupported technology is reported honestly. AI Change Review is optional and hidden/disabled by default where configured; AI is not the core workflow.</p></section>
    <section id="optional-capabilities" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Optional and extension capabilities</h2><p>Feature Visibility can hide or expose optional navigation items. AI Change Review is optional, may be disabled by default and should not be read as a required workflow step. Extensions such as M2LB child-security-classification apply only when that project extension is present and enabled.</p></section>
    <section id="feature-details" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">Feature details and current limitations</h2><p>Each matrix entry opens the actual current page. Page-level help and result provenance describe that feature’s inputs and limits; use those details for target-specific prerequisites. Documentation Health checks structured coverage and route facts but cannot certify prose quality.</p></section>
    <section id="about" class="ug-section"><h2 class="ug-section-title">About</h2><p>BirkNext helps teams inspect evidence and decide what to investigate. Its reports are bounded by available analyzers, providers, configured targets and recorded evidence. Absence of an automated violation is not full compliance, and suggested evidence is not confirmed evidence.</p></section>
</div>

@code {
    private sealed record Link(string Id, string Label);
